{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh7P5dMzXMK51S1aepjU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For octahedral d⁸ (³A₂g ground state), I used the Tanabe–Sugano / Racah energy expressions for the spin-allowed triplet bands.\n\n**1. Dq from the first band**\nFor d⁸ the energy of ³T₂g measured from ³A₂g is exactly 10Dq. No configuration interaction enters this band.\n10Dq = 10 600 cm⁻¹, so **Dq = 1060 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. B from the two ³T₁g bands**\nThe two ³T₁g states, ³T₁g(F) and ³T₁g(P), mix with each other. Their energies above the ground state are:\n\nE(³T₁g) = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½√(225B² − 180·B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n\nAdding the two roots removes the square root:\nν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B\n17 500 + 27 600 = 45 100 = 31 800 + 15B\n**B ≈ 887 cm⁻¹**\n\n**3. Checks**\n- Free-ion Ni²⁺ has B ≈ 1040 cm⁻¹. So β = 887/1040 ≈ 0.85, which is a normal nephelauxetic reduction for NH₃.\n- The three bands do not fit two parameters perfectly. Fitting ν₂ alone gives B ≈ 1080 cm⁻¹, and fitting ν₃ alone gives about 850 cm⁻¹. A least-squares fit to all three bands gives Dq ≈ 1085 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 824 cm⁻¹, with residuals of about 250 cm⁻¹. The sum rule above is the standard way to treat this data.\n- The spin-allowed triplet bands depend only on Dq and B, not on C. So the data cannot determine C. The value C = 4.5B ≈ 3990 cm⁻¹ comes only from the ratio given in the question.\n\n**Result:** Dq = 1060 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 887 cm⁻¹, and C ≈ 3990 cm⁻¹ (from the assumed C/B = 4.5).\n\n{\"Dq\": 1060, \"B\": 887, \"C\": 3990, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq comes from nu1 = 10Dq, B from the sum nu2+nu3 = 30Dq+15B, and C only from the assumed C/B = 4.5.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1215,"cache_read_input_tokens":1710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1215,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard",